 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "According to the National Environment Agency (NEA), the haze in Singapore may see some slight relief in wind transport on **Sunday, October 18**, though overall air quality will remain poor and is not expected to clear up significantly. \n\nDetails from the NEA's forecast for Sunday include:\n\n* **Air Quality (PSI):** The 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) is forecast to remain in the **150 to 220 range (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy)**. This is a very slight drop from Saturday's forecast range of 150 to 230, and compares to Saturday evening's actual readings of 130 to 202.\n* **Weather and Winds:** Showers are expected over Singapore and the surrounding region. Prevailing winds are forecast to shift to blow from the south-southeast or south. \n* **Smoke Transport:** While this wind shift is expected to reduce the amount of thick smoke haze blowing in from Kalimantan, the change in direction means some smoke haze from Sumatra may drift toward Singapore instead.\n\n**Looking Ahead (Monday & Tuesday):**\nConditions have a better chance of gradually improving early next week. More showers are forecast over both Singapore and parts of Kalimantan on Monday and Tuesday, with winds expected to weaken further, which should continue decreasing the transport of smoke from Kalimantan. \n\n**Health Advisory:**\nBecause PSI levels will still sit in the Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy bands, the NEA advises healthy individuals to avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion. Vulnerable groups—including the elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic lung or heart conditions—should minimize or avoid outdoor activity. If you need to go outside, check the live 1-hour PM2.5 readings on the NEA website (haze.gov.sg) or the myENV app for immediate conditions.",
